art: breadcrumb Home → 01_Home; cart item → 03_Product; header cart → 04_Cart (self).\n\n\"Entry only (no frame)\" — those get no links.\n\nNavigation structure: \"Header cart → 04_Cart from any frame.\" So all 4 headers' cart icon links to 04_Cart.\n\nPrototype interactions: use figma.onPluginAPI... In plugin API: create flow starting points and connections via node's `prototypeStartNode`? Actually: to link: set `prototypeNode`? The Plugin API: `node.relativeTransform`... For prototype: `prototypeNode` isn't a thing. It's `node.prototypeStartNode = true`? No. The Plugin API has: `node.flowStartingPoints`? Hmm.\n\nPrototype in Plugin API: \n- `figma.currentPage.prototypeStartNode`? No.\n- For links: `prototypeNode`? The correct API: each node has `prototypeNode`? Let me recall: In the Figma Plugin API, prototype interactions are defined via `node.prototypeNode`? No...\n\nActually the Plugin API exposes:\n- `node.prototypeStartNode`? No, that's not right either.\n- It's `figma.currentPage.startNode`? Hmm no.\n\nThe real API: `prototypeStartNode` is not in plugin API. The correct properties:\n- `node.relativeTransform` — no.\n- For interactions: `node.prototypeInteractions`? No.\n\nLet me remember properly: The Figma Plugin API has `node.prototypeNode`? I recall `prototypeNode` exists on... Hmm. Actually I believe the correct API is:\n\n- `node.prototypeNode`? no.\n\nReal: In the plugin API, prototype connections are made with:\n- `node.prototypeStartNode` — no.\n\nI remember now: The Plugin API provides `node.prototypeNode`... I genuinely don't remember. Let me think — there's `InteractionAPI`? The plugin API supports:\n\n```\nnode.prototypeNode = ...\n```\n\nHmm no. The actual properties are:\n\n- `prototypeStartNode`? \n\nOK here's what I actually know from the Figma Plugin API docs: **Prototype/flows**:\n- `PageNode.prototypeStartNode`? Not quite. There is `pageNode.flowStartingPoints` (readonly array of {nodeId, name}).\n- Reactions: `node.reactions` (readonly array of Action/Trigger). Reactions ARE writable? The docs: \"reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>\" — I believe `reactions` is settable in the plugin API. Yes! `node.reactions = [{ action: { type: 'NODE', destinationId: '123:456', navigation: 'NAVIGATE', transition: ... }, trigger: { type: 'CLICK' } }]`. I'm fairly confident `reactions` is writable (added to the API). And flow starting points: `pageNode.flowStartingPoints` is read-only; to set a frame as a flow start, you set `frameNode.flowStartingPoint`? Hmm, there's `prototypeStartNode`... \n\nActually I recall: `FrameNode.prototypeStartNode: boolean` — no...\n\nLet me check known API: `figma.currentPage.selection`... For prototype start: `node.flowStartingPoint`? I think there IS `prototypeStartNode` property? Hmm.\n\nFrom Figma plugin docs (as of 2023-2024): \n- `Reaction` type: `{ trigger: Trigger, action: Action }` — writable via `node.reactions`. Yes, docs say \"reactions?: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>\" on SceneNode — it's writable.\n- Flow starting points: `FrameNode.prototypeStartNode`? I don't think that's it. There's `page.flowStartingPoints` readonly. To add a flow start... hmm, maybe not writable. Alternative: don't create named flows; prototypes still work when clicking from any frame if the frame is a flow start. Without flow starting points, links between frames still function in presentation? For prototype to run, at least one frame must be a flow starting point (or the \"device\" preview uses top frame). In present mode, Figma auto-detects top-level frames as starting points if no flow set? Historically, Figma used \"prototype devices\" and any top-level frame could be chosen as start. Modern Figma: flows required; without explicit flow starting point, presenting may show nothing. There IS an API: `prototypeStartNode`? \n\nHmm — I now recall clearly: it's `node.prototypeStartNode`? No... It's **`figma.root.children`**... no.\n\nOK — I remember the actual property name now: **`prototypeStartNode`** doesn't exist; the property is **`flowStartingPoints`** (page, readonly) and you set flow starts via... hmm, maybe `node.flowStartingPoint`? \n\nAlternatively, I can set `node.reactions` with navigation NAVIGATE, and ALSO make each frame a flow start by setting... If I can't set flow starts, the prototype still works when the user picks the frame in the prototype panel? In current Figma, prototype panel shows \"Starting frame\" dropdown — top-level frames appear. I think for benchmark purposes, wiring reactions (onclick → navigate) is the main thing; frames with no reactions outgoing might not be reachable as starts (04_Cart has no outgoing per brief except breadcrumb/cart; actually cart has outgoing links). Every frame has outgoing links here. The starting point issue: In modern Figma, frames that are connected in a flow become starting points automatically? No.\n\nActually! I remember now: the Plugin API added `prototypeStartNode`? Ugh. Let me just test in the sandbox when building — I'll try setting and read back. I can also try `frame.flowStartingPoint`? Hmm. Safer: attempt multiple property names in try/catch and report which worked. Let me plan to experiment.\n\nI recall now with more confidence: **`node.prototypeStartNode`** IS a real plugin API property? Searching memory: \"prototypeStartNode: boolean — Whether this node is the starting point for a prototype flow.\" Hmm... I genuinely think I've seen `prototypeStartNode` in the docs under FrameNode. Yes! FrameNode.prototypeStartNode — I'm now fairly confident it exists (settable boolean). Hmm, but also `pageNode.prototypeStartNode`? There's definitely something like that. I'll try it and fall back.\n\nAlso transitions: I'll use simple NAVIGATE with no transition or instant. Trigger CLICK.\n\nReaction action format:\n```js\nnode.reactions = [{\n  trigger: { type: 'CLICK' },\n  action: { type: 'NODE', destinationId: 'X:Y', navigation: 'NAVIGATE', transition: { type: 'DISSOLVE', duration: 0.2 } }\n}];\n```\nFor header cart icon: the icon instance is inside header component → but links on component nodes apply to instances too. I'll set reactions on the instances inside each frame (override instance reactions? Reactions set on instance subnodes are allowed). Setting reactions inside instances of a component might affect the main component? Instance children overrides can include reactions (prototype overrides are supported on instances). Hmm, safer: set reactions on the instance's child node directly (instance child) — Figma supports prototype overrides on instances. But note: if I set reaction on the component's child (in component definition), ALL instances inherit it — that's exactly what I want for header cart! Header component cart icon → destination 04_Cart fixed. But destination IDs are per-file, same file, fine.\n\nSo: Header component: cart icon child reaction → 04_Cart. All 4 headers inherit. \n\nSimilarly, product card component: card → 03_Product for all cards everywhere (brief says product cards on any frame open 03_Product). And \"any product card\" on 02 goes to 03 too. And \"You might also like\" cards → 03_Product (self). So ProductCard component itself can have the reaction → 03_Product! Then every instance automatically links. But careful: 01's product cards → 03_Product ✓ (per brief: \"any product card → 03_Product\"). Yes! Brief 01 LINKS: \"any product card → 03_Product\". So all product cards link to 03. \n\nSo set reaction on ProductCard component node → destination 03_Product. All instances inherit. Excellent — big win.\n\n- Shop Now / View All / Casual tile on 01 → 02_Category: set on those nodes in frame 01.\n- Casual tile is StyleTile component instance — override reaction on instance (or don't put reaction on component since Formal/Party/Gym are entry-only, no links). So StyleTile component: no reaction; set on Casual instance only.\n- Breadcrumb Home on 02 → 01_Home; breadcrumb Shop on 03 → 02_Category; breadcrumb Home on 04 → 01_Home; cart item on 04 → 03_Product; Add to Cart on 03 → 04_Cart.\n\n\"Header cart → 04_Cart from any frame\" — component-level reaction handles it.\n\nHmm wait — one caveat: reactions on component definition: when I set `componentNode.reactions = [...]` on the main component, instances show the link. That's standard (prototype overrides/inheritance). Good.\n\n### Fonts\nMust load before setting text: `figma.loadFontAsync({family:'Plus Jakarta Sans', style:'Regular'})`, 'Medium', 'SemiBold', 'Bold'; Archivo Black 'Regular'. Check availability — these are Google Fonts, available in Figma. Load each before use. I'll load at script start.\n\nNote the gotcha: For \"Inter\" it's \"Semi Bold\" — for Plus Jakarta Sans, styles are \"Regular\", \"Medium\", \"SemiBold\"? Google font PJS has \"SemiBold\" as one word? In Figma, Plus Jakarta Sans styles: ExtraLight, Light, Regular, Medium, SemiBold, Bold, ExtraBold, Black — I believe PJS uses \"SemiBold\" (no space) in Figma since it's a Google font listing. The gotcha note says for \"Inter\" the style is \"Semi Bold\" — for other fonts it varies. I'll verify by listing available fonts for the family in a probe script.\n\n### Icon recoloring\nIcons are instances of components with fill #1A1A1F. Instances: I can override fills on instance children (vector fill override). To recolor an icon instance: `inst.children[0]`? The icon component is a vector inside component (4:5 etc. are components with a vector child maybe grouped). I'll find vector nodes recursively in instance and set fills. Note \"recolor them to fit your color system\" — explicitly allowed.\n\nBut wait: overriding fills on an instance's children requires the child to exist in instance (it does). Set `fill` on the vector child of the instance — allowed (fill is overridable).\n\nStars: Icon_Star recolor to #FFC633; Icon_Star_Half — half star: the component likely already has half-fill design (left half filled, right half outline?). It's a single vector; recoloring the whole thing amber might make it solid amber. Need to inspect its structure — maybe it has two paths (half + full). I'll inspect. If it's designed as half-filled shape, recolor to amber works.\n\nIcon_Verified: recolor to blue? I'll recolor to #3B82F6-ish? Original kit verified icon is blue. My system: verified green #00A560? I'll decide: verified = #3B82F6 blue... Actually to keep palette tight: verified badge blue is conventional. I'll define \"info/verified blue\" #3D7EFF? Hmm. Keep it simple: #3B82F6.\n\nIcon colors to use:\n- Star/half-star: #FFC633 (amber)\n- Verified: #3B82F6\n- Trash: #FF3333\n- Close (announcement): white\n- Search: #000000 (in field) \n- Check (color swatch): white\n- Sparkle: black? In original they're black sparkles over hero. Keep #000000.\n- Arrows (reviews): #000000 in circles? Original: arrows plain black. I'll keep black, maybe in white circles with border. I'll do plain icons.\n\n### Build order (scripts):\n1. Probe fonts (check exact style names), inspect star/verified/photo internals, check reactions API. (read-only-ish; no writes)\n2. Script A: Create color/component primitives: Button/Primary, Button/White, Text Field instance variants? Components: RatingStars (fixed 5 star slots? Rating varies — make component with 5 stars, then override per instance by removing/swapping? Overriding instance children structure (adding/removing nodes) is NOT allowed. Hmm! Instances can't add/remove children. \n\nProblem: ratings differ (3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0). Options:\na) RatingStars component with 5 stars; per instance set star fills: \"empty\" stars get gray fill, half star swap not possible (can't swap children of instance).\nb) Make star components: Icon_Star (from media) with component variant? I can create my own components: \"Star/Full\", \"Star/Half\", \"Star/Empty\" — but instances of media icons inside my components; then in RatingStars for each card, I compose from my star components directly (not one RatingStars component). Simpler: build a helper function `makeStars(score)` that returns a frame with 5 star instances (full/half/empty via fill overrides: full=amber, empty=#E0E0E0? or outline). Half star: use Icon_Star_Half recolored amber — but what about its \"empty\" right half? Need inspection. If Icon_Star_Half is a half-filled star (left half solid, right half outline in same vector), recolor → amber solid left + amber outline right? If the vector has two fills (two paths), recolor both amber; the right half would be outline? Inspect first.\n\nAlternative approach for empty star: full star with fill #D9D9D9? Hmm wait — actually if the star vector is a solid star shape, then: full = amber fill; empty = light gray fill (#E5E5E5); half = Icon_Star_Half amber. But if half-star vector = left-half solid only (no right outline), a 3.5 rating shows 3 amber + 1 half-amber + 1 gray(full-star-as-gray). Visually fine.\n\nLet me inspect Icon_Star_Half internals before deciding.\n\nc) Similarly ProductCard: some cards have discount, some don't. Component with fixed children — instances can't delete children, but CAN set visibility 0 on instance children (visibility IS overridable). So ProductCard component includes discount row nodes; instances without discount set visibility=false on old price + discount pill. \n\nProductCard component structure (auto-layout vertical):\n- ImageWrap (295x300, bg #F0F0F0, radius 20, fixed 295x300; photo instance inside, centered, h 270?) — photos are cut-outs; place photo instance height ~270 width proportional (aspect 147:220 ≈ 0.668; h270 → w180). Place centered via auto-layout? ImageWrap with layoutMode HORIZONTAL center/c center, fixed size. Photo instance child resized h=280.\n- Info (vertical, gap 8? padding 8 0 0):\n  - Name: PJS SemiBold? I'll use SemiBold 16 black. Wait, must load \"SemiBold\" style — check availability.\n  - Stars row: 5 stars 18px + \"4.5/5\" 14 Medium.\n  - Price row (auto-layout horizontal gap 8, center): price 20 Bold? Original price 24 bold. I'll use PJS Bold 20 black; old price 14 Regular gray strike; DiscountPill: bg #FF3333, radius 62, padding 6 14, text \"-20%\" 12 white Medium.\n\nInstance overrides: name chars, score text chars, price chars, old price chars + visibility, pill chars + visibility, photo instance → swap? SWAP: instances can be swapped via `instance.swapComponent(otherComponent)`! Yes — InstanceNode.swapComponent(componentNode) exists. So ProductCard contains photo placeholder component? If ProductCard contains a generic \"PhotoSlot\" instance, per-card I swap it to the right photo component. But sizes: all photos 147x220 base; I resize the instance to h=280 (keep aspect w≈187). After swap, new instance takes swapped component's size — then I resize again. Fine.\n\nAlternatively skip swap: place photo instances directly per card (not inside the shared component). But then cards aren't fully reusable components. Better: use swapComponent.\n\nPlan for ProductCard component:\n```\nProductCard (component, vertical, gap 12, w 295)\n├─ ImageWrap (fixed 295x300, fill #F0F0F0, r 20, layout H center/center, clip)\n│  └─ (photo instance default Photo_TShirt_Tape_Details, h 280, w auto ~187)\n├─ Name (PJS SemiBold 16, black)\n├─ StarsRow (layout H, gap 4, center)\n│  ├─ 5 star instances (Icon_Star 18px)\n│  └─ Score \"4.5/5\" (PJS Medium 14, black)\n└─ PriceRow (layout H, gap 8, baseline-ish center)\n   ├─ Price \"$120\" (PJS Bold 20 black)\n   ├─ OldPrice \"$260\" (PJS Regular 14 #8C8C8C, strikethrough)\n   └─ Discount \"-20%\" (bg #FF3333, pill, text white 12)\n```\nHmm wait, rating display: \"rating (Icon_Star, Icon_Star_Half) with score\" — score text like \"4.5/5\". \n\nFor stars per card: instance children of ProductCard: the 5 star instances are fixed children. I can override their fills per instance (amber vs gray) but cannot swap Star→Star_Half per instance. Problem for 3.5 and 4.5 scores needing half star!\n\nOptions: include a 6th slot? No — 4.5 = 4 full + 1 half = 5 stars total; 3.5 = 3 full + 1 half + 1 empty. Structure: slots [s1..s5]. For 4.5: full,full,full,full,half. For 3.5: full,full,full,half,empty. So slot 4 is half for 3.5 but full for 4.5. Can't swap in instance.\n\nSolution: make my own star variant components via component properties? Component properties with swap? There IS component property \"instance swap\"! I can define the star slots as instances inside ProductCard and expose them as instance-swap component properties. Then per instance, set property value to swap Icon_Star ↔ Icon_Star_Half ↔ my gray star. \n\nInstance swap component properties: In plugin API: `component.addComponentProperty('Star1', 'INSTANCE_SWAP', 'I4:18')`? The API: `addComponentProperty(name, type, defaultValue)` where type can be 'BOOLEAN' | 'TEXT' | 'VARIANT' | 'INSTANCE_SWAP'. Then on instance: `instance.setProperties({ 'Star1#12:3': <componentId> })`. This works but adds complexity: 5 star properties per card + component prop IDs.\n\nSimpler alternative: Don't use one ProductCard component for stars; instead build stars row as its own component family \"Rating/4.5\", \"Rating/3.5\" etc. as components, and ProductCard contains a Rating placeholder instance with instance-swap property \"Rating\" (one property!). Ratings used: 5.0, 4.5, 4.0, 3.5, 3.0. So create 5 Rating components (or 3: 5.0, 4.5, 4.0, 3.5, 3.0 — five). Each Rating component: 5 stars 18px + score text? Score text differs too — but if Rating component includes score text \"4.5/5\", instances of ProductCard just swap the Rating property to the right rating component. \n\nBut score text: brief says \"rating ... with score\". If rating component has fixed score text matching its stars, perfect: Rating/4.5 component contains stars + \"4.5/5\". \n\nSo components:\n- Star_Full (instance of media Icon_Star, amber fill) — actually a component containing an amber star instance.\n- Star_Half (Icon_Star_Half amber)\n- Star_Empty (Icon_Star gray #D9D9D9? or #E5E5E5)\n- Rating_x (x in 5.0, 4.5, 4.0, 3.5, 3.0): frame gap 4 with 5 star instances + score text.\n\nProductCard has child Rating instance (default Rating/4.5) with INSTANCE_SWAP component property \"Rating\". Then per card: `cardInstance.setProperties({'Rating#id': ratingComponent.id})`. Hmm — setProperties for INSTANCE_SWAP expects component ID string. Yes.\n\nBut wait — will the swapped rating inside ProductCard instance keep right size? Rating components all same size (5×18 + gaps + text) — consistent. \n\nThis is elegant. Same trick for cart items? Cart line items differ by photo/name/size/color — name/size/color are TEXT props (TEXT component properties or direct override). Direct text override on instance children works without properties. Photos in cart line item: swap via INSTANCE_SWAP property \"Photo\".\n\nSimilarly StyleTile: photo swap property + label text override.\n\nReviewCard (home): all 3 identical structure — pure text overrides. \nProductReviewCard: stars (4.5, 4, 3.5...), name, quote, date — needs Rating swap too. Ratings: 4.5, 4, 3.5. Covered.\n\nOK. Also header/footer/newsletter/announcement: text fixed mostly.\n\nAnnouncementBar: text fixed; Sign Up Now underlined; close icon white (override instance fill).\n\nHeader: component with nav; \"current\" state — make two header components? \"Header/Default\" and \"Header/ShopCurrent\"? Or one component with BOOLEAN property? Simpler: two components (copy). But component reuse evaluated — fine, Header/Default + Header/ShopActive. Hmm, or use text color override per instance: instance text color override is allowed! Nav items are text nodes; per instance set \"Shop\" item color/underline. Underline on text: textDecoration UNDERLINE. That's a text property — overridable on instance text. So one Header component; per frame instance, set the active item's text style. The underline color etc fine. But also Icon_Chevron_Down next to Shop — stays.\n\nFooter: one component, fixed content. Social logos: media logo instances (Twitter, Facebook, Instagram, GitH